he Committee on Energy and Commerce, and in addition to the Committees on the Judiciary, and Ways and Means, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Abortion","Appropriations","Assault and harassment offenses","Comprehensive health care","Consumer affairs","Crimes against women","Employee benefits and pensions","Government buildings, facilities, and property","Health care costs and insurance","Health care coverage and access","Health facilities and institutions","Health programs administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S000522","cosponsorCount":109,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/7","industries":[{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly restricts what health insurance plans may cover and how federal subsidies interact with abortion coverage in qualified health plans."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill prohibits federal health care facilities and federal employees from providing abortion services, affecting facilities that currently receive federal funds."},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Organizations focused on reproductive health policy and abortion access or restrictions have a direct stake in the regulatory framework this bill establishes."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would make permanent the restrictions on federal funding for abortion services and health insurance coverage that includes abortion. Currently, these restrictions are renewed each year through appropriations bills; the bill would apply them across all federal programs and funds. It would also prohibit qualified health plans offered through insurance marketplaces from covering abortion. The bill includes exceptions for cases of rape, incest, or when the pregnant person's life is in danger.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys health insurance. Anyone who pays a medical bill.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"B001260","score":0.061260165720508956},"overlapCount":107},"meaning":{"percentile":61.74475469316927,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[{"kind":"state-industry","text":"Insurance is also the top donor sector for 2 other members from FL. That points to the industry being big in the state, not to anything about this member."}],"unattributedShare":0.6382143639234255}},{"billId":"119-hr-27","bioguideId":"F000472","cycle":2026,"score":0.00760403307353063,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":12000,"donorShare":0.018376413261032357,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.00760403307353063}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6382143639234255},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-27","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"27","title":"HALT Fentanyl Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Received in the Senate and Read twice and referred to the Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Crime and Law Enforcement","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Department of Justice","Drug trafficking and controlled substances","Licensing and registrations","Research administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"G000568","cosponsorCount":59,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/27","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill changes registration and licensing requirements for pharmaceutical researchers working with controlled substances and affects the regulatory classification of fentanyl-related compounds."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The bill establishes new criminal penalties and quantity thresholds that will affect defense attorneys and prosecutors handling drug trafficking cases."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would permanently classify all fentanyl-related substances as Schedule I controlled substances, meaning they would be treated as having high abuse potential and no accepted medical use. It sets the same criminal penalties and quantity thresholds for fentanyl-related substances as currently apply to fentanyl analogues. The bill also creates a streamlined registration process for researchers studying Schedule I substances and makes it easier for registered researchers to conduct certain activities without additional permits.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People caught up in the criminal law, and victims of crime.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001086","score":0.08496351694573398},"overlapCount":58},"meaning":{"percentile":36.85123836567282,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6382143639234255}},{"billId":"119-hjres-38","bioguideId":"F000472","cycle":2026,"score":0.003133961951493891,"matches":[{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":7000,"donorShare":0.010719574402268875,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.002492924279597413},{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":1000,"donorShare":0.0015313677717526963,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.0006410376718964776}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6382143639234255},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-38","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"38","title":"Providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"Phasedown of Hydrofluorocarbons: Management of Certain Hydrofluorocarbons and Substitutes Under the American Innovation and Manufacturing Act of 2020\".","introducedDate":"2025-02-07","latestActionDate":"2025-02-07","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Energy and Commerce.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"D000628","cosponsorCount":17,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/38","industries":[{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Manufacturers of refrigeration, air conditioning, and cooling equipment would be directly affected by changes to HFC management and recycling requirements."},{"industry":"waste-water","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"The rule establishes recycling standards for spent refrigerants, directly affecting waste management and recycling operations for these materials."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Electric utilities often use HFC-based cooling systems in power generation facilities and would be subject to the emission reduction program."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution would overturn an Environmental Protection Agency rule that manages hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs), which are potent greenhouse gases used in refrigeration and air conditioning. The EPA rule, published in October 2024, sets up a program to reduce HFC emissions and establish recycling standards for spent refrigerants. If this resolution passes, that rule would be nullified and would have no legal effect.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People living near whatever is being regulated.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"W000814","score":0.029638927045184185},"overlapCount":18},"meaning":{"percentile":14.292475